  question-1 v1 question [open] to implementation {"asks":"The markdown rule and the planned node rule disagree on what is an end line. hasMarkerLine/splitAsAsked match only the exact *…* end sentence; the planned ADF reader matches any paragraph whose plain text is the end sentence, whatever its marks. Words containing that sentence plain or as _…_ get sealed (not a marker to sealForFiling), then the node reader closes the region early, the digest misses, and the tail is served as below-the-line, silently. A markdown-level checkAsAsked alone will not catch it; descriptionRoundTrip only does if it runs the same node reader. Which rule closes the gap?","options":["make hasMarkerLine also catch the plain-text / _…_ form, so such words are never sealed as-is","node rule takes the LAST matching paragraph before non-region content"],"recommended":"decide before writing the reader, and have descriptionRoundTrip run the same node reader either way","if-unanswered":"the reader ships with a silent early-close on words that quote the end sentence"}
  finding-32 v1 finding [open] to implementation {"says":"The adf.js header claim that the D1 gate never flags what the writer emits is false. An empty fence (``` then ``` on the next line) makes markdownToAdf emit an empty codeBlock; adfHasUnrenderableContent refuses it, and rt(rt(x)) !== rt(x) (both checks false, observed at 4b5001ddf). Once Harbour writes the block itself, it would create a ticket whose description can never be written again: every later description/below write 422s on D1, which sees the whole document including the block. The planned fixed-point test fails on this input unless the codec is fixed or the input is excluded; pick one deliberately. The plan says D1 unchanged."}
  finding-33 v1 finding [open] to implementation {"says":"The bulk search path stays display-only, so one issue reads differently through search than through getIssue. Check that no cache or guard compares the two."}
  finding-34 v1 finding [open] to implementation {"says":"Atlassian states the entity-property limit in bytes or characters depending on the page. JSON escaping inflates words, so the size guard must measure the serialized property string, not words.length."}
  finding-35 v1 finding [open] to implementation {"says":"Region reader, no second ADF walker: it needs a paragraph's plain text, and `lib/providers/jira/adf.js` already has `plainText` (~line 340), private. Export it rather than retype it, and render the region with `adfToMarkdown({type:"doc",content})`, not a new renderer."}
  finding-36 v1 finding [open] to implementation {"says":"The reader accepts more than the writer refuses: ending the region at a paragraph whose plain text is the end sentence "whatever its marks" means a plain paragraph of that sentence inside the words ends the region early, and `hasMarkerLine` does not refuse it. Safe only if `descriptionRoundTrip` runs through the same reader, so such a filing reads non-kept and goes in unsealed. Add one witness for it."}
  finding-37 v1 finding [open] to implementation {"says":"Scatter: `lib/providers/jira/index.js` has 8 `client.getIssue` sites @4b5001ddf. Four never read a description (703, 1018, 1076, the pre-read at 1099). `fetchIssueFields` (818) feeds `_toCanonicalIssue` but is not in the plan's list. Write one read-with-words helper beside the reader instead of threading `properties` through each call (this also covers plan-review note 2's re-reads)."}
  finding-38 v1 finding [open] to implementation {"says":"What has to learn, beyond the three headers the plan names (every change names what it retires): `dispatch-factory.js:1008`; `docs/proxy-integration.md:2070` (agent-facing, says Jira files unsealed); `docs/architecture/source-map.md:216` and `:82` (the new region module needs a mention); `tests/unit/as-asked-guard.test.js` Jira block (345, 351) will fail, so it notices; the docs will not."}
  finding-39 v1 finding [open] to implementation {"says":"A branch left without a witness: `tests/unit/as-asked-create-seal.test.js:207-227` uses Jira's round trip as the provider that cannot hold the block. After the flip, `sealForFiling`'s `reason: "provider"` branch has no subject in production or tests. Witness it with a synthetic lossy round trip (recommended), or retire it on purpose. `lib/as-asked.js`'s "When the unsealed branches could go" drops "Jira today" but stays, because of older tickets."}
  finding-40 v1 finding [open] to implementation {"says":"Two stores of the words (ADF region plus `harbour.asAsked`, a digest deciding between them) stay safe only while the region module is the one place that knows both. Put the one size function (plan-review note 3) in that module too, so `descriptionRoundTrip`, `updateIssue` and `createIssue` all reach it there."}
  finding-44 v1 finding [open] to implementation {"says":"Do this tidy first, before the seal: an empty fenced code block in the person's words currently files a Jira description that refuses every later write below the line. Fix it so such words file a description that still takes below-the-line writes; that write is what the seal exists to allow."}
4 standing judgements and decisions:
  decision-1 v1 decision [stands] {"answer":"Size: just the task. Cuts: none.","why":"Jira gains issue creation and a description that keeps the person's words byte-exact through later writes, which opens all three filing lanes; the Surveys' converter, rich-content and capability findings are nearby but none binds on this ask. Decided by: the standing policy."}
  decision-2 v1 decision [stands] {"answer":"Shape B: the exact words live in a Jira issue entity property `harbour.asAsked = { v: 1, words, rendered }`; the description holds an ordinary block (an "As asked" level-2 heading, the words converted by `markdownToAdf`, and the italic end line). On read, when the region still renders to `rendered`, the provider serves the exact `words`; when it doesn't, it serves what the region now renders (Linear parity).","why":"B is the only shape that is exact for agents *and* leaves Jira looking like Jira for its people. The second point decides it, because the first-dispatch seal will touch tickets the team wrote themselves. A (a code-block carrier) is a close second and cheaper: every ticket a human wrote in Jira would become a grey raw-Markdown box. C (a normalised baseline) is cheapest, but the stored words would no longer be what the person typed, and it needs a ruling from John; A and B don't, so no ruling is asked for. A lossless codec is ruled out: ADF marks accept no custom attributes, and Jira validates them. What would change it: John's site refuses `properties` in create/edit bodies (then a separate `PUT /issue/{key}/properties/harbour.asAsked`, still B); John wants the ask shown verbatim as code (then A); John rules render-exact acceptable (then C is cheaper)."}
  decision-3 v1 decision [stands] {"answer":"Session fit: needs multiple sessions.","why":"Session 1 holds the region reader's edge cases and the property semantics across client, fake, four reads and the writer; it lands and is verified alone, and when it lands the first-dispatch seal begins sealing Jira-native tickets on John's site. Session 2 holds createmeta, three lanes, the UI flip, and retirements across six docs and prompt files with a prompt-budget re-measure. Session 3 needs a credential no build session has by default. Split at those seams. Each piece lands and verifies on its own, and sessions 2 and 3 are each blocked by the one before."}
  decision-4 v1 decision [stands] {"answer":"plan-review due: yes","why":"more than one session, writes to a real Jira in use, and it changes the sealing contract on a path that rewrites human-authored tickets."}
  verdict-1 v1 verdict [stands] {"of":"plan-review","outcome":"approve","reasoning":"Verdict: Approve. The plan's premises check out at HEAD, it opens all three filing lanes with one provider method plus an honest round trip, and its safeguards hold. The notes are corrections the builder can make without changing the plan's shape. For this piece: the seal module needs no change (`providerHoldsAsAsked`, `lib/as-asked-seal.js:57`, is a synchronous `checkAsAsked(sealed, descriptionRoundTrip(sealed))`, so a pure write-then-read round trip that includes the property turns sealing on); the proxy guard (`routes/proxy-writes.js:336/534`) compares the live `issueDescription` with the new body, so serving exact `words` from the reader is all the guard needs; the D1 premise holds (13 bodies through `adfHasUnrenderableContent(markdownToAdf(sealAsAsked(x)))`, none trips D1; the codec reached a fixed point after one pass on every sample); landing session 1 alone on a live site is safe (a failed first-dispatch seal is caught and logged, `dispatch-factory.js:1066-1079`, and only never-dispatched issues are sealed). Notes 2, 3 and 6 are folded into this version."}
  verdict-2 v1 verdict [stands] {"of":"plan-review","outcome":"approve","reasoning":"Verdict: Approve. The plan's premises check out at HEAD, it opens all three filing lanes with one provider method plus an honest round trip, and its safeguards hold. The notes are corrections the builder can make without changing the plan's shape. For this piece: every lane ends in `provider.createIssue` after `sealForFiling` (proxy create `proxy-writes.js:445/470`, session `workspace-api-issue-write.js:792`, feedback `workspace-api-feedback.js:511`). Staleness: `git log --since=2026-11-13T12:08Z` is empty for the paths involved at `4b5001ddf` = origin/main. Notes 4 (the Periodical report gate never fires on Jira) and 5 (`parentId` is not reachable through the lanes as planned) are folded into this version."}
  verdict-3 v1 verdict [stands] {"of":"plan-review","outcome":"approve","reasoning":"Verdict: Approve. The plan's premises check out at HEAD, it opens all three filing lanes with one provider method plus an honest round trip, and its safeguards hold. The notes are corrections the builder can make without changing the plan's shape. For this piece: against what was asked, the plan delivers every clause with no narrowing; if the builder has no credential for John's site, session 3 is a handoff to John, not a cut. Open questions resolved: the property limit is 32768 characters per Atlassian's entity-property docs (the builder confirms it); whether John's site accepts `properties` in create and edit bodies, its createmeta, and its required fields stay with session 3's live check, with the separate-PUT fallback and the naming 422 already planned. Note 1 (make the live check able to fail) is folded into this version."}
  decision-5 v1 decision [stands] {"answer":"Plan-review due: no.","why":"Covered by LIN-4445's approving plan-review (comment 61e84035-1df2-459f-8def-5afdc7001e64, rev 1). Grounding commit `4b5001ddf` (origin/main when the plan was written and reviewed; re-checked at breakdown on 2026-11-13, with no commits on the plan's paths since)."}
  decision-6 v1 decision [stands] {"answer":"Session fit: fits one session.","why":"Session 1 holds the region reader's edge cases and the property semantics across client, fake, the four reads and the writer, and it lands and verifies alone."}
  decision-11 v1 decision [stands] {"answer":"Size: a small tidy. Cuts: none.","why":"Jira holds the person's words byte-exact (plan-1 session 1, the seal), plus one tidy done first: an empty fenced code block in the words must no longer file a description that refuses every later write below the line, which is the write the seal exists to allow. Decided by: the standing policy; the system holds no setting record for that policy, so this decision rests on nothing it can link."}
